## sess122 — blocker 2 design settled, header half landed

**Build: VERSION `0.11.441`, srcversion `AC4899E877E72D996AEF7B0` → `1D16B8E6FA1DD5BDF32BD17`. `make modules` exits 0, no errors, no new warnings. Still STOP-SHIP; fleet stays on 0.11.440.**

I continued D-SAMENODE-WAITER-CANCEL-COLLISION (ledger #16) rather than starting at #1, same reason as sess119–121 — the tree is mid-landing under the sess118 stop-ship ruling and leaving it half-wired is strictly worse than finishing it.

**The design for blocker 2 is settled.** The sess121 ruling listed 8 required properties; the one that collapses most of the work is that **the obligation's target is the resource, and the slot is derived rather than stored**. That kills ruling item 6 — the single overwriteable `owed_slot` that could lose an obligation's location — because a location re-resolved every pass cannot be overwritten. It also means one owed record per entry suffices instead of a multi-target array. It is the ruling's own accepted design (1), and its two conditions come out clean: what *authorizes* a clear is the plan evaluated at CAS time (no live local attempt, no live tenure ⇒ our bits on this resource are stale by definition), not the slot's incarnation, so the tombstone→recreate ABA the ruling flagged is answered by the guard that already exists.

Two details worth the ink. **Termination needed one exception to "never retract a refused bit":** a holder refusal caused by a live local tenure must discharge, because that bit is authorized rather than dirt and its owner's unlock clears it — without the exception the worker spins forever on a bit that can never become clearable. And **the "no live slot" case is the terminal transition** ruling item D demanded; canonical resolution is what makes it reachable at all.

**What landed is the header half only** — `ctx->owed_worker`, seven counters, the worker's constants, and `lreq_cond` re-documented as the owed-cleanup wake channel. Nothing uses them yet.

I hit the relay boundary mid-edit, having already deleted the CLRWAIT constants and counters that `lreq_finish` still references. Rather than leave a non-building tree, I restored them and marked them for deletion by the `.c` half. The full implementation spec — pure `lreq_plan`, intent-first publication atomic with the clear window, gen-guarded retraction, the dedicated worker with its claim/pin/backoff discipline, the teardown drain, and the two divergence sites that also need obligations — is in ccmemory `ccloop-c7ee71c6-sess122-blocker2-design-and-header-landed`.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready — 29 open defects, 17 critical, and the DLM core is mid-landing under an active stop-ship ruling. The marker stays unwritten.

